{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 17, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e4b-it", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli ruled the County of Tripoli from 1154 until his death in 1199. He was a significant figure in the Crusades and a powerful ruler in the Levant during a turbulent period. His reign was marked by military campaigns diplomatic maneuvering and efforts to consolidate his control over the region. Raymond III inherited the County from his father Raymond II a ruler known for his strength and resilience.\n\n*Early Life and Ascension to the County*\n\nRaymond III's early life was shaped by the ongoing Crusades and the complex political landscape of the Levant. He participated in military expeditions alongside his father gaining valuable experience in warfare. Upon his father's death in 1154 Raymond ascended to the County of Tripoli facing immediate challenges from Muslim forces and rival Crusader states. He quickly established himself as a capable leader defending Tripoli against attacks and strengthening its defenses. His early years focused on securing the county's borders and maintaining stability within its territory. He engaged in alliances with other Crusader rulers like Bohemond of Antioch and Raymond of Antioch to counter threats from the Zengid dynasty.\n\nRaymond III's reign witnessed significant military activity. He fought numerous battles against Saladin and other Muslim commanders. He also engaged in campaigns against the Byzantine Empire seeking to expand his influence. His military successes helped to solidify Tripoli's position as a major power in the region though it remained vulnerable to larger forces. He was known for his strategic acumen and his ability to adapt to changing circumstances.\n\n*Diplomacy and Alliances*\n\nRaymond III was a skilled diplomat who understood the importance of alliances in maintaining power. He forged strategic relationships with various Crusader states and even with some Muslim rulers. He sought to balance his relationships carefully ensuring that no single power became too dominant. He understood that cooperation was essential for survival in the hostile environment of the Levant. He frequently engaged in negotiations and treaties to secure favorable terms for Tripoli. These diplomatic efforts helped to protect Tripoli's interests and maintain its independence.\n\nHis political strategies involved careful calculations of power and a willingness to compromise when necessary. He recognized the limitations of Tripoli's military strength and relied on diplomacy to achieve his goals. He often used marriage alliances to strengthen his relationships with other rulers. These alliances provided mutual support and helped to prevent conflicts.\n\n*Legacy and Death*\n\nRaymond III died in 1199 after a long and eventful reign. He left behind a county that was relatively stable and prosperous. He was remembered as a strong and capable ruler who defended Tripoli against numerous threats. His reign was a testament to his leadership skills his military prowess and his diplomatic abilities. His efforts to maintain Tripoli's independence and prosperity had a lasting impact on the region. He is considered one of the most important rulers of the County of Tripoli. His legacy continued to influence the county's politics and military affairs for decades after his death. His reign provided a crucial period of stability amidst the ongoing conflicts of the Crusades.\n", "logprobs": [{"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -0.0021344278939068317}, {"token": "▁III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Count", "logprob": -2.50339189733495e-06}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁ruled", "logprob": -0.2371193766593933}, {"token": "▁the", "logprob": -0.20648594200611115}, {"token": "▁County", "logprob": -0.054002758115530014}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁from", "logprob